 781 00000734: 7F120000 00000000        move    dsa2+0 to dsa2 with carry
 782 0000073C: 7F130000 00000000        move    dsa3+0 to dsa3 with carry
 783                             
 784                             ; If an i/o request specifies an odd number of bytes and it is a 16-bit scsi
 785                             ; device, getting the last byte to the target is a problem.  The 720 will not
 786                             ; send the last byte unless it is paired up with another to fill the 16-bit
 787                             ; pathway.  If a solution is implemented, it will be done by the host when it
 788                             ; creates the last scatter gather entry in the list.  The command should
 789                             ; contain the actual byte count (an odd value, otherwise it's no problem) but
 790                             ; the last scatter gather entry should have one added to it so an even number
 791                             ; of bytes are "sent" to the target.  The target will dutifully ignore the
 792                             ; extra eight bits since it knows they aren't part of the command.  Failure
 793                             ; to pad with an extra byte would make the 720 hang waiting for the target to
 794                             ; phase change while the target is waiting for the last byte.
 795                             ;
 796                             ; The bottom line is the 720 code does nothing special for this case.
 797                             ; There's nothing for the 720, except this long comment.
 798                             
